Phase 11: Regime-based weighting engine
This is the core of your request. Assign base weights by macro regime, then adjust them dynamically based on recent model performance and confidence. Performance-based dynamic weighting is a common ensemble principle, especially when different models dominate in different conditions.

Base weights by regime

| Regime | Wavelet Pro | HMM Pro | LSTM | TFT |
| ------ | ----------- | ------- | ---- | --- |
| Growth | 30%         | 15%     | 30%  | 25% |
| Normal | 25%         | 25%     | 20%  | 30% |
| Crisis | 15%         | 35%     | 10%  | 40% |

Why these weights
Growth regime: Wavelet Pro and LSTM get the highest weights because cleaner trends, structure, and continuation patterns are more usable, while HMM remains useful as a filter and TFT adds horizon planning.

Normal regime: The system should be balanced, with TFT slightly leading because mixed-input multi-horizon forecasting is especially useful when markets are neither clearly smooth nor clearly broken. HMM and Wavelet stay important because regime and structure still matter.

Crisis regime: HMM Pro and TFT dominate because crisis markets are driven by abrupt regime changes and extreme uncertainty; HMM helps detect latent state transitions, while TFT’s quantile ranges and attention over important stress periods help more than plain sequence memory. LSTM gets the lowest base weight because sequential assumptions tend to fail more often during shocks.

Phase 12: Dynamic weight adjustment
Do not keep fixed weights inside each regime. Instead, start from the base table and adjust using four multipliers:

Recent model performance multiplier

last 20 trades or rolling out-of-sample block

Sharpe, hit rate, calibration, and drawdown contribution

Confidence multiplier

Wavelet structural clarity

HMM state certainty

LSTM forecast confidence

TFT quantile narrowness

Disagreement penalty

if one model strongly disagrees with the other three, reduce its live weight

Regime suitability multiplier

each model has a historical scorecard by regime
For example, if LSTM historically underperforms in Crisis, cap it even when recent signals look strong.

Example live formula
Professional ensemble score should conceptually be:

Final Weight = Base Regime Weight × Performance Multiplier × Confidence Multiplier × Suitability Multiplier

Then normalize all weights so total weight remains 100%.

Phase 13: Meta-decision layer
After weights are computed, the system should produce:

final direction score,

final confidence score,

recommended position size,

no-trade flag,

stop/target class.

Decision logic example
If final score is strongly bullish and confidence is high, allow full-size long.

If bullish but driven only by one model with weak confirmation, reduce size.

If Crisis regime is active and TFT uncertainty is very wide, block new trades even if direction is positive.

This avoids the common mistake of letting every model trigger trades independently.

Phase 14: Position sizing by regime
Risk should also change by regime.

| Regime | Typical position sizing policy                    |
| ------ | ------------------------------------------------- |
| Growth | normal to moderately aggressive                   |
| Normal | balanced and confirmation-driven                  |
| Crisis | defensive, smaller size, stricter no-trade filter |

In practice:

Growth: allow full size if Wavelet + LSTM + TFT align.

Normal: require at least two strong models and acceptable HMM stability.

Crisis: require HMM and TFT agreement plus controlled uncertainty; otherwise trade small or stay flat.

